A last written lsn per block, in shared memory.

A read of a page names an lsn and the page service waits until ingest
has walked past it. Until now that lsn was one number for the whole
cluster, the durable end of the stream, so a backend reading a block
nothing had touched in an hour still waited for every commit any other
backend had just made. The wait is real work and it shows up as park
time on the read tier.

The fix is the pair Neon carries: a request says both where the reader
stands, which is the tenant wide watermark, and where this block was
last written. The service can answer as soon as ingest is past the
second one, because everything after it belongs to some other block.
Correctness rests on the block position being an upper bound on the
last change to the block, never a lower one.

This is where the upper bound comes from. A block dirty in shared
buffers is read out of the buffer and never reaches the smgr, so the
only blocks a read can ask for are blocks that have been written back,
and a writeback flushes the block's wal first. That makes the page lsn
at writeback time at or above the last record that touched it, which
is what zouwritev and zouextend record here.

The table is a direct mapped array of positions in shared memory with
no keys in it, sized by ZOU_LWLSN_ENTRIES and off entirely at zero. A
slot holds the newest position of every block that hashes to it, so
two blocks sharing a slot cost a wait for each other's writes rather
than a wrong answer, and they cost it to one block in nslots rather
than to all of them. A slot at zero means nothing hashing there has
been written back since startup, and those read at a floor seeded once
from GetFlushRecPtr, which is at or past the last change to every
block already written back, for the same wal before data reason.

The keyless shape is the second attempt. The first kept a key per slot
and folded an evicted block's position into the floor, and under a
write heavy run the evicted positions are the ones written a moment
ago, so the floor climbed to within a hair of the newest lsn and every
read waited there. On gamingpc it measured the same as not having the
table at all, 373 tps on the write mix and 12604 on the read one.

Recovery is carved out. The startup process needs the page at exactly
the position it is replaying, not at some later one, so ZouLwLsnRange
returns InvalidXLogRecPtr under RecoveryInProgress and the read keeps
naming ZouWalReadLSN, which is the interlock zou #329 put in.

Pages with an invalid lsn are not wal covered and go to the store
eagerly, so they need no slot and no wait, and the note skips them.

The knob is an environment variable rather than a GUC because that is
what every other zou setting in this tree is, ZOU_TARGET and
ZOU_PAGESERVE and the rest. zou #697.

